# AI Bridge — universal AI-to-computer control platform (v0.3.0)

Connect authorized AI assistants and coding agents (Claude, Codex, Gemini
CLI, other MCP clients, and ChatGPT via supported remote MCP) to Linux
and Windows computers their owners authorize them to control — through
one consistent, policy-enforced MCP tool interface.

**This is not a remote root shell.** There is no `run_any_command`, no
arbitrary PowerShell endpoint, and no tool that can grant itself
privileges. See `docs/security.md` and `docs/threat-model.md` first.

## What v0.3 does (security batch)
- **MCP server** (stdio + Streamable HTTP): system info, resources,
  workspace list/read/search/create/edit/move/delete, service status,
  logs, processes, disk/memory, Docker, git, tests, linter, backup,
  staging deploy, task submit/status/result/cancel, approvals, desktop
  (opt-in), device list/status.
- **Risk tiers, enforced server-side**: 1 read-only, 2 low-risk,
  3 sensitive (opt-in or approval), 4 high-risk (always human approval).
- **Remote control plane** (FastAPI): device enrollment with a
  one-time token (hashed at rest), revocation, task queue, agent polling
  (outbound only, bounded backoff), approvals, audit log, emergency stop.
- **Linux agent** (systemd, unprivileged) and **Windows agent**
  (service account + separate interactive desktop helper).
- **Dashboard** (React/TypeScript): user login or admin token, devices, tasks, approvals,
  audit, emergency stop, versioned policies.
- **Security batch**: expiring/rotatable device tokens, local users and roles, versioned policies,
  Ed25519 signed-update verification, sandboxed task execution.
- **Tests**: unit, integration, security, and end-to-end (see below).

## Quick start (local MCP, Linux)
```bash
python3 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e ".[dev]"
mkdir -p "$HOME/ai-bridge-workspace"
export AI_BRIDGE_WORKSPACE="$HOME/ai-bridge-workspace"
pytest -q
python -m ai_bridge.server   # stdio; driven by your MCP client
```
MCP client config: copy `integrations/generic-mcp/mcp-config.json` and
replace the paths. Per-client configs: `integrations/{claude,codex,gemini,chatgpt}/`.

## Control plane + dashboard
```bash
export AI_BRIDGE_ADMIN_TOKEN="$(python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))")"
python -m ai_bridge.control          # :8000, loopback
# dashboard (separate terminal):
cd apps/dashboard && npm install && npm run dev   # :5173
```
Enroll a device in the dashboard, copy its one-time token, and start an
agent: `python agents/linux/agent_main.py --device-token <token>`.
Full steps: `docs/installation-linux.md`, `docs/installation-windows.md`,
`docs/remote-access.md` (TLS proxy — required for any remote exposure).

## Repository layout
```
src/ai_bridge/        core: security, policy, workspace, diagnostics, execution, tools, server, control/
agents/               linux/ windows/ common/ (poller)
apps/                 control-api/ (uvicorn entrypoint)  dashboard/ (React+TS)
integrations/         chatgpt/ codex/ claude/ gemini/ generic-mcp/
deployment/           docker/ systemd/ linux/ windows/
docs/                 architecture, threat-model, security, install, mcp-clients, remote-access, operations, troubleshooting, api-reference, implementation-status
migrations/           001_initial.sql (Postgres mirror of the SQLite schema)
tests/                unit/ integration/ security/ e2e/ (+ starter tests in tests/*.py)
```

## Gap analysis vs. the starter ZIP (Milestone 0)
The starter (10 files, ~300 lines) had only a stdio server with 4 tools
(`system_info`, `list_workspace`, `read_workspace_file`,
`run_diagnostic_task`), fixed read-only diagnostics, and path tests.
It lacked: writes, policy tiers, approvals, audit persistence, remote
enrollment/control plane, dashboard, Windows agent, desktop control,
HTTP transport, rate limiting, redaction, and backups. All of those are
added in v0.2 except the items honestly marked incomplete in
`docs/implementation-status.md` (signed updates, OIDC, macOS, Windows
execution on a Windows host, Postgres runtime wiring).

## Safety notes
- Run as an unprivileged user. Never run the agent as root/SYSTEM.
- Keep secrets, SSH keys, `.env`, and production data OUTSIDE the workspace.
- Never paste tokens or secrets into chat; use `.env` with `chmod 600`
  (see `.env.example`) or a secret manager.
- The starter's original files were preserved and hardened, not deleted:
  `security.py` (now with secret-name blocking + redaction),
  `tasks.py` (now a compatibility shim), `server.py` (now the full tool set).
